    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run.entry_resolve", decl_name: "ProviderLayoutAnswer", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run.entry_resolve", decl_name: "CoreResolve", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run.entry_resolve", decl_name: "resolved_graph_from_sources_with_index_typed", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run.entry_resolve", decl_name: "resolve_entry_typed", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run.emit_host", decl_name: "fixture_pool_answer", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run.emit_host", decl_name: "fixture_pool_closure_of", field: WholeDeclaration }
  ],
  reason: "WHY RUST RATHER THAN .dag: the closure builders the seed runs -- the entry-resolve core behind claim runs, claim_batch, whole-tree compile and demand-acquired `gunbc run`, the emit-check fixture path and the hermetic fixture -- are hand-Rust over host storage (MultiEntryIndex, the witness-root module index, a manifest). Acquiring a module means reading that storage and re-running the seed's own resolve, which the v1 interpreter cannot express as a .dag fold. Every decision stays in the model: which provider is owed (the demand inference emits), whether a declaration is satisfied, absent or refused, whether to acquire, complete or refuse (std.generated_provider provider_acquisition_step via v1.compiler.infer closure_provider_acquisition_step), and the refusal itself (CompilerDiagnostic GeneratedProviderUnavailable). No builder rescans conversion_plan_rows, chooses a provider, or keys acquisition on a carrier. WHY ADMITTED AGAINST THE v1 FREEZE: gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing v1_seed_standing admits by PURPOSE -- the host-text crossing cut (N7, the eight tests reaching their verdicts through v2-emitted Rust) needs the seed to acquire the route its generated call names, and the alternative was a near-global carrier-presence dependency the operator rejected.",
  owning_dissolution_lane: "v1-hand-queue-drain" as RoadmapNodeId,
  trigger: "Delete these declarations when the native compiler acquires a compiler-generated declaration's provider through v2.compiler.module_acquisition (the N2b generated_head_demand route extended to infer-known demands, consuming std.generated_provider), SUFFICIENT FOR the host-text crossing controls and the N7 tests to reach their verdicts without v1_compiler.cli_run.provider_acquire. WHAT DOES NOT RETIRE IT: moving the loop into a different seed file; a carrier-keyed pre-acquisition; a fallback to the old chars spelling call.",
  current_boundary: "v1_compiler.cli_run.entry_resolve resolved_graph_from_sources_with_index settles generated providers after reconcile and before the type verdict, recursing on the grown closure; on the demand-acquired `gunbc run` path (MultiEntryIndex provider_layout set) a provider the layout holds and the acquired closure does not comes back from the typed core (resolved_graph_from_sources_with_index_typed, CoreResolve NeedsAcquisition) with the exact source the layout answer read, and v1_compiler.cli_run.demand_acquire resolve_entry_graph_demand_acquired rebuilds the closure from that snapshot (acquire_layout_closure_seeded); v1_compiler.cli_run.emit_host compile_fixture_rendering_only_what_is_read (emit-check, pool: witness-root module index) and compile_dag_multi_module_fixture (hermetic, pool: empty) go through compile_to_provider_fixed_point. Controls: test.claim.generated_provider_step (the model) and the three provider_acquire tests (supplied demand through each real adapter)."
}
